// expect: 3
// RENAMING A PARAMETER CHANGES THE VERDICT. `twice(f, x)` names its first
// parameter `f`, which is also a top-level function. The free-name walk
// reads a function's body with its parameters unbound, so `f` inside
// `twice` becomes a call edge to the top-level `f`, the two land in one
// binding group, and `twice` is judged monomorphically: six E_TypeMismatch
// errors (measured 2026-09-25). The same program with the parameter named
// `g` checks clean and answers 3.
//
// Retires when the call graph is read from resolved references, so a
// parameter is never an edge to a namesake (Hβ.resolve.reference-is-an-edge).
fn f(x) = {
  let a = twice(inc, x)
  let b = twice(flip, true)
  if b { a } else { 0 }
}

fn twice(f, x) = f(f(x))

fn inc(n) = n + 1

fn flip(b) = !b

fn main() = f(1)
